   "passage": "Adding pivot-filtered back-translation to Wickersham-MT improved average BLEU by 3.1 points over genuine parallel data alone (from 12.0 to 15.1) across the four low-resource pairs, a smaller gain than the 5.8-point average improvement previously reported for the technique by Rasmussen-Obi & Petrakis (2023). Pivot-filtered back-translation also outperformed unfiltered back-translation on average BLEU (15.1 vs 13.4) and average chrF (42.3 vs 40.3), with gains on every individual pair for both metrics. The default pivot round-trip threshold of 0.4 yielded the highest average BLEU (15.1) among the three thresholds tested (0.2: 14.3; 0.6: 14.8), while the number of synthetic pairs kept decreased as the threshold increased (3.0M, 1.8M, 1.1M). Wickersham-MT with pivot-filtered back-translation achieved a higher average BLEU (15.1) than the external Pellston-NMT system (14.5) across the four pairs, though Pellston-NMT scored higher on en→si (15.0 vs 14.2).",
